      "id": 1,
      "text": "East Springfield’s Arch Bridge, built in 1832, spans the local creek and remains a rare 19th‑century stone arch in Otsego County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://springfieldhistoricalsocietyny.org/our-town/",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Hyde Hall, constructed between 1817 and 1833 by George Clarke, is a grand estate that now serves as a historic house museum.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://springfieldhistoricalsocietyny.org/our-town/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s oldest golf course, established in 1894, sits on land once owned by Leslie Pell Clarke and Henry L. Wardwell.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://springfieldhistoricalsocietyny.org/our-town/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A 12‑foot‑deep sinkhole known as the Chyle Hole circles about 80 yards, a curious limestone feature that locals still explore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://springfieldhistoricalsocietyny.org/our-town/",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Tin Top Gatehouse, once the entrance to Hyde Hall’s grand estate, still stands as a distinctive 19th‑century landmark.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
